dzn: Keep the driver's compiled pipeline state objects in the disk cache

The disk cache keeps the NIR and DXIL of pipelines, but creating each PSO
still ran the GPU driver's compiler in every new process: about 25 PSOs and
700 ms of compiling while Android started on an Intel Iris Plus 650 under
WSL2, where D3D12 reports D3D12_SHADER_CACHE_SUPPORT_SINGLE_PSO and no
automatic cache.

After compiling a PSO, store its GetCachedBlob() in the disk cache, keyed
by its pipeline state stream as the in-process PSO cache keys it, with the
root signature identified by a hash of its serialized form, which Dozen now
attaches to the root signatures it creates. Before compiling one, look the
key up and pass a stored blob as the stream's CACHED_PSO; if the driver
does not accept it, compile as before.

Drivers that report D3D12_SHADER_CACHE_SUPPORT_DRIVER_MANAGED_CACHE, a
shader cache of their own, are left to it: under WSL2 NVIDIA's and AMD's
do (an RTX 4080 Laptop GPU and a Radeon 780M), Intel's UHD Graphics does
not. NVIDIA's GetCachedBlob there (driver 32.0.15.9579) frees memory it
did not allocate, and glibc aborts the process ("double free or
corruption"): crosvm's GPU process died as gfxstream created its first
pipeline, and Android never started.

